record.content:
This is a contributed topic on representations of canonical commutation and anti-commutation relations.
\subsection{Representations of Canonical Commutation Relations (CCR)}
\subsubsection{Canonical Commutation Relations:}
Consider a Hilbert space $\H$. For a linear operator {\bf O} on $\H$, we denote its domain by {\bf $D(O)$}. With \PMlinkexternal{Arai's notation}{http://planetphysics.org/?op=getobj&from=lec&id=124}, a set {Q_j , P_j}^d _{j =1} of self-adjoint operators on $\H$ (such as the position and monetum operators, for example) is called a \emph{representation of the canonical commutation relations (CCR) with $d$ degrees of freedom} if there exists a dense subspace $\mathcal{D}$ of $\H$ such that: \begin{itemize} \item (i) $\mathcal{D} \subset \bigcap^d_{j,k=1}[D(Q_jP_k) \bigcap D(P_kQ_j)\bigcap D(Q_jQ_k) \bigcap D(P_jP_k)],$ and \item (ii) $Q_j$ and $P_j$ satisfy the CCR relations:
$$[Q+j , P_k] = i\delta_{hjk}$$, $$[Q_j , Q_k] = 0, [P_j , P_k] = 0$$, $j, k = 1, · · · , d$, on $\D$, where $\hbar$ is the Planck constant $h$ divided by $2 \pi$. \end{itemize}
A standard representation of the CCR is the well-known Schr\"odinger representation $\left\QSj, PS \right\}^d_j=1 $ which is given by: $$\H = L_2(Rd),QS_j= x_j, $$
the multiplication operator by the j-th coordinate $x_j$ , with $PS_j = −i \hbar D_j$ , with $D_j$ being the generalized partial differential operator in $x_j$ , and $JD = S(Rd)$ being the Schwartz space of rapidly decreasing $C_1$ functions.
\subsubsection{CCR Representations in a Non-Abelian Gauge Theory} One can provide a representation of canonical commutation relations in a \PMlinkexternal{non-Abelian gauge theory}{http://planetphysics.org/?op=getobj&from=lec&id=124} defined on a non-simply connected region in the two-dimensional Euclidean space. Such representations were shown to provide also a mathematical expression for the non-Abelian, Aharonov-Bohm effect.
\subsection{Canonical Anti --Commutation Relations (CAR)}
